format session-log-v1
stamp 20260821.202424
editor Claude Code
model claude-opus-5
voice Kyri
host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title One list, in one file, that both tools read
prompt carry the exclusion list into one place both tools read
think Checked the two lists before unifying them and found they had ALREADY diverged again -- within the hour of booking %121 for exactly this. The census had gained shipped_binary_claim_control.sh and the repointer had not. Twice is carelessness; three times inside a day is structure, and structure is what a shared file fixes.
think Wrote the shared file to carry the reasoning rather than only the names, since the next person adding an entry needs the test more than the list. The test is one sentence: is this the field, or the instrument?
think Emitted the list three ways rather than one, because grep and find want different shapes and a shared list that only serves one consumer is a shared list with a favourite.
think Disabled globbing around every expansion, since dated_path_* is a pattern meant for grep and find rather than one the shell should resolve against whatever directory a caller stands in. That is not hypothetical -- run from tools/fixtures it would have matched real files and silently changed meaning.
think Confirmed the refactor was behaviour-preserving before believing it: the census printed the same 176 gone and 192 lost afterward, digit for digit.
obs The unification immediately found something neither private copy had: a PATH dimension. docs-geode/demos/README.md demonstrates the resolver recovering a stale reference, which requires quoting one, and it prints the answer it gets -- so the repointer wanted to rewrite it, which would have made the page's own quoted output wrong. Its basename is README.md, which no name-match could exempt without exempting every README in the tree.
obs Made a regression and caught it in the same pass. Sourcing the shared file by a relative path broke the census's own control corpus, which runs the scan from a throwaway tree. It resolves from the script's own directory now -- and the sha3 shim taught this identical lesson hours earlier, which is a small argument for reading the tree before adding to it.
obs Added the assert that makes the rule real rather than aspirational: both tools must source the shared file, and neither may carry a hand-rolled exclusion. Proven by refusing a planted copy that grows one back, and by accepting the real two, which is the half that keeps a rule from becoming a nuisance.
loom exclusions=unified file=1 consumers=2 forms=3 names=4 paths=1
loom census=unchanged gone=176 lost=192 ceiling=192 before_and_after=identical
loom repointer=dry would_repoint_before=1 after=0 the_one=demos_page_demonstration
loom guards=17 green=17
file tools/fixtures/dated_path_exclusions.sh the one list, with the test that decides what joins it
file tools/fixtures/dated_path_scan.sh sources it; grep filters the path roster it cannot exclude
file tools/fixtures/dated_path_repoint_scan.sh sources it; find excludes names and paths directly
file tools/fixtures/dated_path_exclusions_control.sh a planted private copy, refused; the real two, accepted
file tools/dated_path_witness.rish the single-list rule asserted rather than hoped
file crux/REDS.md %121 CLOSED -- one list, and what unifying it found
recommend check-in (checkpoint) all seventeen guards GREEN, the two tools read one list, and the rule that keeps them together is asserted rather than remembered. The pattern worth carrying beyond this pair: every exclusion list in this tree is a claim about what is not the field, and any such list kept in two places is two lists that happen to match today.
